From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 09:26:05 +0200
Subject: ath10k: keep the firmware regdomain when the calibration declares none

Devices which ship calibration data with a zeroed regulatory domain field
report it in the WMI SERVICE_READY event as "eeprom_rd 0x0" and leave the
choice of the firmware regulatory domain to the driver. ath10k derives it
from ath_common, so as soon as a country is configured the shared ath regd
code maps that country onto a regional SKU and ath10k hands the firmware
its conformance test limit (CTL) selection along with it:

  wmi pdev regdomain rd 37 rd2g 37 rd5g 37 ctl2g 30 ctl5g 30 dfs_region 2

ctl2g 0x30 (CTL_ETSI) directs the firmware to apply the ETSI 2.4 GHz CTL
table from the board data file. On the AVM FRITZ!Box 7590 (two QCA9984,
firmware 10.4-3.9.0.2) that table is not usable and the firmware applies
it without validation: the 2.4 GHz gain state ends up corrupted, the per
chain noise floor lands 13-33 dB hot and asymmetric, energy detect asserts
on an idle channel for ~99.9% of the airtime, the transmitter never keys
and the firmware cycles DEV_TX_TIMEOUT -> DEV_RESET every 0.7 s forever.
The state survives interface bounces, firmware redownload and cold boots,
because the same regdomain is reprogrammed at every firmware start. Three
different board data files including the vendor's own behave identically,
so this is a property of the board data lineage, not of one file. The
5 GHz radio of the same device takes ctl5g 0x30 without trouble; only the
2.4 GHz table is broken.

When the calibration data declares no regulatory domain, program the
firmware with the regpair table's own "no regulatory domain" entry
(NO_ENUMRD, CTL DEBUG_REG_DMN) rather than with whatever a country hint
remapped ath_common to, on every regulatory update and across firmware
restarts. Regulatory enforcement stays with cfg80211 and mac80211, which
apply the configured country's channels and power limits host side.

Programming a *world* SKU instead - for instance the FCC3_FCCA pair that
ath/regd.c derives for rd 0 by way of its CTRY_UNITED_STATES fallback -
cures the 2.4 GHz jam just as well, but is not a correct fix: this
firmware validates the channel of every vdev start against the programmed
regulatory domain, so under an 11 channel domain a device configured for
an ETSI country loses channels 12 and 13 device side. The radio is then
healthy, but starting an AP on channel 13 fails with
"vdev-start-response reports status error: 1" (chan-invalid) and the
interface never comes up. The no-regdomain entry does not constrain the
channel set, which is what makes it the right thing to send.

Regulatory enforcement itself does not change. cfg80211 and mac80211 keep
applying the configured country's channel set and power limits host side
(a device configured for DE keeps its 13 channels at the ETSI limits), and
the DFS pattern detector keeps following the country as well. The
dfs_region argument of WMI_PDEV_SET_REGDOMAIN is therefore deliberately
left country driven: it selects the firmware's radar detection rules, not
a board data power table, and pinning it would weaken DFS behaviour for no
benefit.

The change is scoped to devices that explicitly declare no regional
binding: the pin is only armed when the calibration regdomain is zero and
no regdomain was derived from elsewhere either, so devices with a real
calibration regdomain and devices which got theirs from ACPI/WRDD keep the
current behaviour.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

Forwarded: no

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/core.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/core.h
@@ -1033,6 +1033,13 @@ struct ath10k {
 	u32 hw_min_tx_power;
 	u32 hw_max_tx_power;
 	u32 hw_eeprom_rd;
+
+	/* Regulatory pair the firmware is pinned to, set only on devices whose
+	 * calibration data declares no regulatory domain (hw_eeprom_rd == 0).
+	 * See ath10k_mac_get_fw_regpair().
+	 */
+	const struct reg_dmn_pair_mapping *cal_regpair;
+
 	u32 ht_cap_info;
 	u32 vht_cap_info;
 	u32 vht_supp_mcs;
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c
@@ -3544,9 +3544,50 @@ ath10k_mac_get_dfs_region(enum nl80211_d
 	return WMI_UNINIT_DFS_DOMAIN;
 }
 
+/* The "no regulatory domain" entry of the shared regpair table, spelled out
+ * here because that table (regd_common.h) is private to ath/regd.c and defines
+ * static data, so it cannot be included from ath10k:
+ *
+ *	regDomainPairs[0] = { NO_ENUMRD, DEBUG_REG_DMN, DEBUG_REG_DMN }
+ *
+ * ath/regd.c never hands this entry out - ath_get_regpair() returns NULL for
+ * NO_ENUMRD, and for calibration data that declares no domain __ath_regd_init()
+ * substitutes the United States regpair instead.
+ */
+static const struct reg_dmn_pair_mapping ath10k_regpair_no_enumrd = {
+	.reg_domain	= 0x0000,	/* NO_ENUMRD */
+	.reg_5ghz_ctl	= 0x01ff,	/* DEBUG_REG_DMN */
+	.reg_2ghz_ctl	= 0x01ff,	/* DEBUG_REG_DMN */
+};
+
+/* Pick the regulatory pair the firmware is programmed with.
+ *
+ * Devices whose calibration data declares no regulatory domain must not be
+ * handed a country derived regional domain, see ath10k_mac_register(). For
+ * those the no-regdomain pair is programmed instead of the pair the country
+ * hint remapped ath_common to. Only what the firmware is told is pinned this
+ * way, the channel list and the power limits keep coming from cfg80211 and are
+ * unaffected.
+ */
+static const struct reg_dmn_pair_mapping *ath10k_mac_get_fw_regpair(struct ath10k *ar)
+{
+	const struct reg_dmn_pair_mapping *regpair = ar->ath_common.regulatory.regpair;
+
+	if (!ar->cal_regpair || ar->cal_regpair == regpair)
+		return regpair;
+
+	ath10k_info(ar, "calibration declares no regdomain, pinning fw regdomain 0x%02x (ctl2g 0x%02x ctl5g 0x%02x) instead of 0x%02x, host regulatory stays in force\n",
+		    ar->cal_regpair->reg_domain,
+		    ar->cal_regpair->reg_2ghz_ctl,
+		    ar->cal_regpair->reg_5ghz_ctl,
+		    regpair ? regpair->reg_domain : 0);
+
+	return ar->cal_regpair;
+}
+
 static void ath10k_regd_update(struct ath10k *ar)
 {
-	struct reg_dmn_pair_mapping *regpair;
+	const struct reg_dmn_pair_mapping *regpair;
 	int ret;
 	enum wmi_dfs_region wmi_dfs_reg;
 	enum nl80211_dfs_regions nl_dfs_reg;
@@ -3557,7 +3598,11 @@ static void ath10k_regd_update(struct at
 	if (ret)
 		ath10k_warn(ar, "failed to update channel list: %d\n", ret);
 
-	regpair = ar->ath_common.regulatory.regpair;
+	regpair = ath10k_mac_get_fw_regpair(ar);
+	if (!regpair) {
+		ath10k_warn(ar, "failed to set pdev regdomain: no regulatory pair\n");
+		return;
+	}
 
 	if (IS_ENABLED(CPTCFG_ATH10K_DFS_CERTIFIED) && ar->dfs_detector) {
 		nl_dfs_reg = ar->dfs_detector->region;
@@ -10361,6 +10406,17 @@ int ath10k_mac_register(struct ath10k *a
 		goto err_dfs_detector_exit;
 	}
 
+	/* Calibration data which declares no regulatory domain states that the
+	 * device is not bound to a region, and the board data of such devices is
+	 * not guaranteed to carry usable CTL tables. Pass that statement on to
+	 * the firmware unchanged instead of letting a configured country select a
+	 * regional domain for it. Regulatory enforcement is not affected,
+	 * cfg80211 keeps applying the country's channel and power limits host
+	 * side.
+	 */
+	if (ar->hw_eeprom_rd == 0 && ar->ath_common.regulatory.current_rd == 0)
+		ar->cal_regpair = &ath10k_regpair_no_enumrd;
+
 	if (test_bit(WMI_SERVICE_SPOOF_MAC_SUPPORT, ar->wmi.svc_map)) {
 		ar->hw->wiphy->features |=
 			NL80211_FEATURE_SCAN_RANDOM_MAC_ADDR;
